From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media, drm: h713: re-arm the capture after the descriptor is published

Until today the picture froze the moment the video plane came up, and the
only way back was a source switch issued from userspace by someone who
happened to know that publishing the descriptor had stopped the capture.
That knowledge does not belong in a userspace recipe.

The chain of facts, all of them measured on the board:

1. Writing the VidDec descriptor stops the capture. The firmware reads the
   write as a VideoDec signal event, sets MemoryAgent +8 and lets
   memory_agent_onoff clear the INCAP output enable, because the capture is
   not in the source mask of VideoDec. INCAP +0x104 goes on counting at 60/s
   and nothing is written -- the wall keeps the last frame it took. Cold-start
   counter-test, doku/nachtlog/A-abnahme-board.md 22:26.

2. A source *change* makes it run again; setting the source it is already on
   does not. On 07.09.2026 three THal_Vp_SetSource(HDMI-1) calls in a row left
   +0x928 bit 31 clear, while VideoDec-then-HDMI-1 set it every time.

3. The firmware answers the second call about half a second before the capture
   is actually running: 539 ms, 536 ms and 544 ms over three cycles, from the
   return of the RPC to the bit going up. Whoever waits for it has to wait for
   the ring, not for the reply.

4. After a cold start the capture is not running at all -- +0x104 does not
   count -- and it took a VIDIOC_S_INPUT to get it going. A device that comes
   up not delivering and waits to be asked is a device with a manual.

So: the display driver announces the publication on a chain of its own, and
the capture driver answers it by switching the source away and back and
waiting for the ring. The same worker runs once at the end of probe, which
covers point 4.

The chain is separate from the flip chain on purpose. The flip chain is the
vsync interrupt: its members are counted and holding one keeps the interrupt
unmasked. A listener for the descriptor event wants neither -- it wants to be
told once, whenever it happens, whether or not anything is streaming. The new
chain is blocking and is called from the atomic commit tail, so a handler may
sleep; this one does not, it hands the half second to a worker.

This also corrects a comment. h713_hdmirx_set_source() claimed that "a real
change away and back does not make the capture run again by itself", citing
doku/78 appendix A.5. That was written before the source switch was measured
(doku/nachtlog/B2-quellenwechsel.md) and re-measured today; it is wrong, and a
wrong comment next to the function that needs it is worse than none.

diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 11:01:17.229896365 +0200
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 11:10:56.544777188 +0200
@@ -512,6 +512,36 @@
 	return h713_afbd_in_ring(h, *y) && h713_afbd_in_ring(h, *c);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The descriptor chain. Separate from the flip chain on purpose: the flip
+ * chain is the vsync interrupt and its members are counted, because holding a
+ * member there keeps the vsync interrupt unmasked. A consumer of the
+ * descriptor event wants neither -- it wants to be told once, whenever the
+ * event happens, whether or not anything is streaming.
+ */
+static BLOCKING_NOTIFIER_HEAD(h713_afbd_desc_chain);
+
+int h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&h713_afbd_flip_lock, flags);
+	ret = h713_afbd_flip_dev ? 0 : -EPROBE_DEFER;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&h713_afbd_flip_lock, flags);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return blocking_notifier_chain_register(&h713_afbd_desc_chain, nb);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier);
+
+int h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
+{
+	return blocking_notifier_chain_unregister(&h713_afbd_desc_chain, nb);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier);
+
 int h713_afbd_register_flip_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
@@ -799,6 +829,7 @@
 	unsigned int w, hgt, c_stride;
 	unsigned long flags;
 	bool ring, nv16;
+	bool published = false;
 	u32 y, c;
 
 	new_state = drm_atomic_get_new_plane_state(state, plane);
@@ -824,8 +855,9 @@
 		 */
 		if (nv16)
 			writel(0, h->regs + AFBD_VIDEO_C_STRIDE);
-		if (h713_afbd_publish_video_info(h, w, hgt, fb->pitches[0]) &&
-		    nv16 && h713_afbd_wait_wce(h))
+		published = h713_afbd_publish_video_info(h, w, hgt,
+							 fb->pitches[0]);
+		if (published && nv16 && h713_afbd_wait_wce(h))
 			drm_err(&h->drm,
 				"firmware did not reprogram the chroma stride within %u ms of the descriptor; source 0 is programmed anyway, so this enable may come up with a halved chroma stride\n",
 				H713_WCE_TIMEOUT_US / 1000);
@@ -903,6 +935,17 @@
 	}
 
 	h->video_active = true;
+
+	/*
+	 * Last, and only if the firmware really got something new: the
+	 * publication above has switched the capture off, and whoever owns the
+	 * capture has to be told so it can switch it back on. Announced after
+	 * the plane is fully programmed, so a listener that reacts at once
+	 * finds source 0 in its final state and not half of it.
+	 */
+	if (published)
+		blocking_notifier_call_chain(&h713_afbd_desc_chain,
+					     H713_AFBD_EVENT_DESCRIPTOR, NULL);
 }
 
 static void h713_afbd_video_atomic_disable(struct drm_plane *plane,
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:01:17.231792126 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 11:10:56.546422263 +0200
@@ -60,6 +60,7 @@
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 #include <linux/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h>
 #include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-arisc.h>
@@ -145,6 +146,32 @@
 
 /* THal_Vp_SetSource: 3 is HDMI-1, the board's only socket (hardware port 0). */
 #define H713_SOURCE_HDMI1	3
+/*
+ * The source the firmware is switched to and away from to make the capture
+ * run again. Any source other than HDMI-1 would do -- what the firmware
+ * reacts to is the change, not the destination -- and VideoDec is the one
+ * that was measured (doku/nachtlog/B2-quellenwechsel.md).
+ */
+#define H713_SOURCE_VIDEODEC	1
+
+/*
+ * How long the capture gets to come back after the source has been switched
+ * away and back. Measured on 07.09.2026 over three cycles: 539 ms, 536 ms and
+ * 544 ms from the return of the second RPC until INCAP +0x928 bit 31 was set
+ * again (doku/nachtlog/E2-freigabe.md). The budget carries the same reserve
+ * the ARISC driver already gives its own firmware waits, and like every
+ * deadline in this tree it is a failure report, never a remedy.
+ */
+#define H713_REARM_TIMEOUT_MS	2000
+
+/*
+ * How often the way back to HDMI-1 is tried before giving up. Three, because
+ * the failure it covers is a single lost RETURN and not a state the
+ * co-processor is stuck in -- if three deadlines of two seconds each pass
+ * without an answer, the problem is not one this loop can fix, and going on
+ * would only delay the log line that says so.
+ */
+#define H713_SOURCE_RETRIES	3
 #define H713_HDMI_PORT		0
 
 /*
@@ -395,6 +422,10 @@
 	const struct h713_hdmirx_slot_source *slot_source;
 	struct notifier_block flip_nb;
 
+	/* re-arming the capture after the display published the descriptor */
+	struct notifier_block desc_nb;
+	struct work_struct rearm_work;
+
 	/* last callback payloads, for debugfs and the source change event */
 	spinlock_t event_lock;
 	u32 signal_args[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS];
@@ -535,11 +566,10 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * Stage 5 of the source change. Two things about it are worth knowing and
- * both are measured: if the firmware's active source already is HDMI-1 this
- * is a null change and moves nothing, which is normal and not an error; and
- * a real change away and back does not make the capture run again by itself
- * (doku/78 appendix A.5, open point A.6 item 1).
+ * Stage 5 of the source change. If the firmware's active source already is
+ * HDMI-1 this is a null change and moves nothing, which is normal and not an
+ * error -- and it is exactly why setting HDMI-1 once is not enough to bring a
+ * stopped capture back. See h713_hdmirx_rearm().
  */
 static int h713_hdmirx_set_source(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
@@ -785,6 +815,145 @@
 	return a.y != b.y;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Switch the firmware's source away from HDMI-1 and back.
+ *
+ * The firmware re-runs its V_INCAP group on a source *change*, which is why
+ * this goes away and back instead of setting HDMI-1 once. Both halves are
+ * needed: measured on 07.09.2026, three THal_Vp_SetSource(HDMI-1) calls in a
+ * row left INCAP +0x928 bit 31 clear, while away and back set it every time
+ * (doku/nachtlog/E2-freigabe.md). That also corrects the note that stood at
+ * h713_hdmirx_set_source() until today; doku/78 appendix A.5 was written
+ * before B2 measured the source switch.
+ *
+ * Sleeps in both calls; process context only.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_switch_source(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	u32 arg = H713_SOURCE_VIDEODEC;
+	unsigned int try;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_call(rx->id_setsource, &arg, 1, NULL, 0, 0);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(rx->dev,
+			"Freigabe: SetSource(VideoDec) fehlgeschlagen: %d -- die Quelle steht unveraendert\n",
+			ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * From here the source is *not* HDMI-1 any more, and a return without
+	 * having put it back leaves the device showing nothing. That is why
+	 * this half is retried and the first half is not.
+	 *
+	 * What is retried is a lost RETURN, not a rejected call: -110 means
+	 * the co-processor acknowledged the message and then did not answer
+	 * within the deadline -- a missing acknowledgement would be -ETIME,
+	 * because that is what down_timeout() returns. It happened once at
+	 * probe on 07.09.2026 and left the source on VideoDec with no picture,
+	 * which is the failure this loop exists for. SetSource is idempotent,
+	 * so sending it again costs nothing if the first one did arrive after
+	 * all.
+	 */
+	for (try = 1; try <= H713_SOURCE_RETRIES; try++) {
+		ret = h713_hdmirx_set_source(rx);
+		if (!ret) {
+			if (try > 1)
+				dev_info(rx->dev,
+					 "Freigabe: SetSource(HDMI-1) im %u. Anlauf angekommen\n",
+					 try);
+			return 0;
+		}
+
+		dev_warn(rx->dev,
+			 "Freigabe: SetSource(HDMI-1) Anlauf %u von %u fehlgeschlagen: %d\n",
+			 try, H713_SOURCE_RETRIES, ret);
+	}
+
+	dev_err(rx->dev,
+		"Freigabe: SetSource(HDMI-1) blieb nach %u Anlaeufen aus -- die Quelle steht auf VideoDec, es kommt kein Bild\n",
+		H713_SOURCE_RETRIES);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Make the capture run again and say whether it worked.
+ *
+ * The wait is not politeness: the firmware answers the second RPC before the
+ * capture is running again, so a caller that returns at once would report
+ * success while the ring still stands still. On the descriptor path it takes
+ * about 25 ms; on a warm bench the INCAP output-enable bit itself came back
+ * 536 to 544 ms after the second reply over three cycles.
+ *
+ * What this criterion cannot separate: a ring that is running again from one
+ * that had not finished draining. It answers the question that matters --
+ * "are frames arriving" -- and it is the only question this driver can ask
+ * without mapping a block that belongs to the capture.
+ */
+static void h713_hdmirx_rearm(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
+{
+	ktime_t start;
+
+	if (h713_hdmirx_switch_source(rx))
+		return;
+
+	start = ktime_get();
+	do {
+		if (h713_hdmirx_signal_present(rx)) {
+			dev_info(rx->dev,
+				 "Freigabe: Capture laeuft wieder nach %lld ms\n",
+				 ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start));
+			return;
+		}
+	} while (ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start) < H713_REARM_TIMEOUT_MS);
+
+	/*
+	 * Not an error return: there is nobody to return it to, and the most
+	 * ordinary reason for it is the most boring one -- nothing is plugged
+	 * in. Said out loud all the same, because if a picture is expected and
+	 * none arrives, this line is where the search starts.
+	 */
+	dev_info(rx->dev,
+		 "Freigabe: nach %u ms bewegen sich die Flip-Zeiger nicht; liegt ein Signal an?\n",
+		 H713_REARM_TIMEOUT_MS);
+}
+
+static void h713_hdmirx_rearm_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = container_of(work, struct h713_hdmirx,
+					      rearm_work);
+
+	h713_hdmirx_rearm(rx);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The display driver has just published the VidDec descriptor. The firmware
+ * takes that write as a VideoDec signal event and answers it by clearing the
+ * INCAP output enable: the capture goes on counting frames and writes none,
+ * so the wall keeps the last picture it took. Measured, not deduced
+ * (doku/nachtlog/A-abnahme-board.md, cold-start counter-test 22:26) -- and it
+ * is why the picture used to freeze the moment the video plane came up, which
+ * userspace then had to undo with a source switch it had no way of knowing
+ * about.
+ *
+ * Handing this to a worker is not a detail: the notifier runs in the display
+ * driver's atomic commit tail, and the re-arm takes about half a second.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_desc_notify(struct notifier_block *nb,
+				   unsigned long action, void *data)
+{
+	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = container_of(nb, struct h713_hdmirx, desc_nb);
+
+	if (action != H713_AFBD_EVENT_DESCRIPTOR)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	schedule_work(&rx->rearm_work);
+
+	return NOTIFY_OK;
+}
+
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
  * Buffers: buffer i is slot i, for the whole life of the queue
  * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
@@ -1557,6 +1726,8 @@
 	rx->dev = dev;
 	rx->slot_source = &h713_hdmirx_vsync;
 	rx->flip_nb.notifier_call = h713_hdmirx_flip_notify;
+	rx->desc_nb.notifier_call = h713_hdmirx_desc_notify;
+	INIT_WORK(&rx->rearm_work, h713_hdmirx_rearm_work);
 	rx->timings = h713_hdmirx_timings;
 	rx->last_slot = -1;
 	mutex_init(&rx->lock);
@@ -1682,6 +1853,21 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_v4l2;
 
+	/*
+	 * From here on the display tells this driver when it has published the
+	 * descriptor, so the capture is switched back on without anyone in
+	 * userspace having to know that publishing it switched the capture
+	 * off. -EPROBE_DEFER cannot happen at this point: the flip read near
+	 * the top of this function already established that the display has
+	 * bound.
+	 */
+	ret = h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier(&rx->desc_nb);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+			      "Descriptor-Notifier nicht anmeldbar\n");
+		goto err_vdev;
+	}
+
 	rx->debugfs = debugfs_create_dir(H713_HDMIRX_NAME, NULL);
 	debugfs_create_file("status", 0444, rx->debugfs, rx,
 			    &h713_hdmirx_status_fops);
@@ -1690,8 +1876,32 @@
 	dev_info(dev, "/dev/video%d, Slot-Quelle: %s\n", rx->vdev.num,
 		 rx->slot_source->name);
 
+	/*
+	 * And once for the state this driver was just booted into. After a
+	 * cold start the capture is not running -- INCAP +0x104 does not count
+	 * at all -- and until today it took a VIDIOC_S_INPUT from userspace to
+	 * get it going (measured 07.09.2026, doku/nachtlog/E2-freigabe.md).
+	 * There is no reason for a device to come up not delivering and wait
+	 * to be asked.
+	 *
+	 * The switch, but not the wait that h713_hdmirx_rearm() does. The two
+	 * cases are not the same question. After the descriptor this driver
+	 * knows the capture was running and knows what stopped it, so a ring
+	 * that stays still is news. Here it knows neither: the hotplug pulse a
+	 * second earlier has just told the source to re-negotiate, and the
+	 * source is entitled to take its time -- on this bench it needs longer
+	 * than the whole re-arm budget. Waiting for it would put a line about
+	 * a missing signal into every normal boot, and a warning that fires
+	 * when nothing is wrong stops being read. The capture comes up on its
+	 * own once the source is back; ENUM_INPUT answers the question for
+	 * anyone who wants it.
+	 */
+	h713_hdmirx_switch_source(rx);
+
 	return 0;
 
+err_vdev:
+	vb2_video_unregister_device(&rx->vdev);
 err_v4l2:
 	v4l2_device_unregister(&rx->v4l2_dev);
 err_callbacks:
@@ -1704,6 +1914,8 @@
 {
 	struct h713_hdmirx *rx = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 
+	h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier(&rx->desc_nb);
+	cancel_work_sync(&rx->rearm_work);
 	debugfs_remove_recursive(rx->debugfs);
 	vb2_video_unregister_device(&rx->vdev);
 	v4l2_device_unregister(&rx->v4l2_dev);
diff -ruN a/include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h b/include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h
--- a/include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h	2026-09-07 11:01:17.228381775 +0200
+++ b/include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h	2026-09-07 11:10:56.543054012 +0200
@@ -33,8 +33,9 @@
 
 struct notifier_block;
 
-/* The only event on the chain today: a capture slot has been finished. */
+/* Events. FLIP travels the vsync chain, DESCRIPTOR the descriptor chain. */
 #define H713_AFBD_EVENT_FLIP		1
+#define H713_AFBD_EVENT_DESCRIPTOR	2
 
 /**
  * struct h713_afbd_flip - the capture ring flip pointers, sampled at one vsync
@@ -107,6 +108,46 @@
  */
 int h713_afbd_read_flip(struct h713_afbd_flip *flip);
 
+/**
+ * h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier() - be told when the VidDec
+ *	descriptor has been published to the firmware
+ * @nb: notifier block; its handler is called with action
+ *	%H713_AFBD_EVENT_DESCRIPTOR and a NULL payload
+ *
+ * Publishing the descriptor is not a private matter of the display driver,
+ * and that is the whole reason this chain exists. The firmware reads the
+ * write as a VideoDec signal event: MemoryAgent +8 goes to 1 and
+ * memory_agent_onoff clears the INCAP output enable, because the capture is
+ * not in the source mask of VideoDec. The capture then keeps counting frames
+ * and writes none -- the wall shows the last one it took
+ * (doku/nachtlog/A-abnahme-board.md, cold-start counter-test 22:26).
+ *
+ * So the one action that stops the capture happens in this driver, and the
+ * one driver that can start it again is the capture driver. Without this
+ * chain that knowledge lives in a userspace recipe, which is where it was
+ * until 07.09.2026 and where it does not belong.
+ *
+ * This is a blocking chain and the handler runs in the atomic commit tail:
+ * it may sleep, but everything it does delays the scanout commit, so a
+ * handler that has real work to do should hand it to a worker.
+ *
+ * May sleep; call from process context.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, -EPROBE_DEFER if the display device has not bound
+ * yet, -ENODEV if the display driver is not in this kernel at all.
+ */
+int h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb);
+
+/**
+ * h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier() - stop being told
+ * @nb: the notifier block that was registered
+ *
+ * Waits for a handler that is currently running to return. May sleep.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, -ENOENT if @nb was not registered.
+ */
+int h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb);
+
 #else /* !CONFIG_DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD */
 
 static inline int h713_afbd_register_flip_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
@@ -123,6 +164,18 @@
 {
 	return -ENODEV;
 }
+
+static inline int
+h713_afbd_register_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
+{
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
+
+static inline int
+h713_afbd_unregister_descriptor_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
+{
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
 
 #endif /* CONFIG_DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD */
 
